WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.800
INTRO:
Do you know who’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.160
the third-largest semiconductor company in the&nbsp;
world? The first two are relatively easy to guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.920
they’re Nvidia and TSMC. But what about the&nbsp;
third one? Some good guesses would be Intel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:18.720
Qualcomm, and TI, but the answer is actually&nbsp;
a company called ASML. Currently, ASML boasts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:24.480
a market cap of just under $300 billion coming&nbsp;
in at the 29th largest publicly traded company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:30.240
in the world. To put that in perspective, their&nbsp;
market cap is currently over twice that of AMD.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:34.720
Aside from a behemoth market cap, in terms of&nbsp;
semiconductor wafer front-end equipment market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.600
share, ASML was the second largest single&nbsp;
market share holder coming in at 15.4%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:46.000
only beaten out by Applied Materials. Yet most&nbsp;
people have never even heard about ASML. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.640
here’s the story of ASML and how they&nbsp;
became the $300 billion shadow chip company.

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:54.880
SHED OPERATION:&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.800
Taking a look back, the story of ASML dates back&nbsp;
to the Netherlands to the year 1984. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.200
many chip companies were trying to commercialize&nbsp;
a manufacturing technique called photolithography.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.160
Photolithography is the process of using light in&nbsp;
order to produce a thin protective film. In terms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:15.200
of semiconductors, the thin film is generally&nbsp;
used to protect select portions of silicon wafers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.600
This technique actually has roots that date&nbsp;
back as far as 1820 to a french inventor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.800
named Nicephore Niepce, but it wasn’t till the&nbsp;
1970s that semiconductor companies really started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:29.760
to leverage this process. In the meantime, two&nbsp;
relatively small companies named Royal Philips&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:34.720
Electronics and Advanced Semiconductor Materials&nbsp;
International, or ASMI, felt that they were being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:39.760
left behind with this process. So, they decided to&nbsp;
make a joint venture called Advanced Semiconductor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:45.520
Materials Lithography or ASML. Despite being&nbsp;
founded by two existing companies, the early days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:51.040
of ASML weren’t glamorous by any means. Philips&nbsp;
cleaned out a shed behind one of their offices and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.040 --> 00:01:56.640
that became the new headquarters of ASML. Over the&nbsp;
next several months, ASML focused on developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:01.280
and integrating photolithography. And given that&nbsp;
they weren’t inventing a new process from scratch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:07.120
it didn’t take them too long to come out with&nbsp;
their first system called the PAS 2000 stepper.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.480
If you’ve ever wondered what the&nbsp;
big machines in chip factories are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:15.200
well, this is one of them. Anyway, with their&nbsp;
first product up and running, the parent companies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.960
decided to up their investments into ASML. By&nbsp;
1985, ASML upgraded to a newly built office and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:27.360
factory located in Veldhoven. Their employee count&nbsp;
also grew to a solid 100 employees. Soon after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.840
ASML came out with their second product in 1986&nbsp;
which was the PAS 2500 stepper. As ASML’s product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:37.360
lineup grew, they started to form partnerships&nbsp;
with established companies such as the lens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:43.200
manufacturer Carl Zeiss. Philips also helped ASML&nbsp;
open locations in Taiwan and the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.560
but all was not good. Here’s the thing, while&nbsp;
ASML was churning out great products, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:53.120
nothing particularly new or innovative. There&nbsp;
was already plenty of comparable solutions from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.080
established semiconductor companies, and customers&nbsp;
saw no reason to take a gamble with a startup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:04.000
So, ASML had high expenses and little revenue. To&nbsp;
make things worse, one of the parent companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.440
ASMI, would call it quits in the mid-1980s. They&nbsp;
sold their 50% stake in ASML to Philips and left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:14.480
the startup. While Philips purchased the stake&nbsp;
from ASMI, they weren’t that optimistic about ASML&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.720
either. Philips’ investors were starting&nbsp;
to suggest that ASML was a lost cause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.200
and that Philips should cut losses as soon&nbsp;
as possible. Considering the dire situation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.200 --> 00:03:26.640
ASML’s executives reached out to&nbsp;
Philips’ board member Henk Bodt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.760
and they were able to come to one final deal.&nbsp;
Philips would back one more round of funding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:37.120
but if that didn’t work or wasn’t enough,&nbsp;
they were going to shut down ASML. Oftentimes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.800
it’s when the stakes are the highest that we&nbsp;
produce the best results, and this couldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.840
be truer for ASML as they came out with a&nbsp;
breakthrough platform called the PAS 5500.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:49.200
BREAKTHROUGH:&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.800
The PAS 5500 boasted industry-leading productivity&nbsp;
and resolution which motivated many customers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.800 --> 00:04:00.080
give the startup a shot, and this is exactly&nbsp;
what ASML needed to finally turn a profit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:06.000
Despite the positive progress though, Philips was&nbsp;
still looking to get out of ASML. So, in 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:10.400
they took ASML public on the Amsterdam and New&nbsp;
York Stock exchanges at roughly a billion-dollar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:15.200
valuation. Philips sold half of their stake at&nbsp;
IPO, and they slowly sold off the rest of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:19.920
stock over the next couple of years. And with&nbsp;
that, Philips was completely out of the business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.280
but that was ok as they had already done their&nbsp;
part in keeping ASML alive. Ever since then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:30.160
ASML has expanded into 3 other categories aside&nbsp;
from lithography which include the track division,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:34.560
the thermal division, and the special applications&nbsp;
division. Starting with the track division, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.360
part of the company focuses on producing equipment&nbsp;
that can coat, develop, and bake light-sensitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:44.480
materials onto a wafer’s surface. Moving onto the&nbsp;
thermal division, this sector produces furnaces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:49.200
and deposition systems. The furnaces are used to&nbsp;
heat up wafers during the manufacturing process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.200 --> 00:04:52.320
and the deposition systems are&nbsp;
used to remove chemical vapors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.320 --> 00:04:57.040
And finally, the special applications division&nbsp;
is basically just a customization department.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.200
This sector customizes products produced by the&nbsp;
other departments to meet customer needs. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:06.720
example, one customer might need larger furnaces&nbsp;
in order to manufacture larger batches of wafers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.600
Similarly, another customer might need smaller&nbsp;
furnaces and so on and so forth. Looking at these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:16.960
divisions, you probably realized that ASML isn’t&nbsp;
actually a chip manufacturer themselves. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:21.040
they primarily deal with manufacturing&nbsp;
chip-producing equipment. So, they’re basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:25.840
the top of the semiconductor food chain. When you&nbsp;
buy an iPhone, Apple turns around and gets Foxconn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:30.800
to manufacture the smartphone. Foxconn then turns&nbsp;
around and calls up TSMC to source the chips.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:36.400
And then TSMC turns around and calls up ASML&nbsp;
to get their equipment. So, though ASML is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.200
mostly invisible to the public, without them,&nbsp;
basically nothing would take place. Anyway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.400
going back to the late 1990s, the dot-com bubble&nbsp;
was in full force, and ASML leveraged the bubble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:50.720
to raise money and continue innovation. They were&nbsp;
no longer innovating to catch up though, now, they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.720 --> 00:05:56.080
were innovating to lead the market. In 1996, ASML&nbsp;
introduced their step and scan system which blew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.080 --> 00:06:01.600
away the competition. This system was capable of&nbsp;
producing 100 wafers per hour which was nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.200
double the standard 60 wafers per hour. Seeing&nbsp;
this, the US Department of Energy invited ASML to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.400
expand its presence in the US through the Extreme&nbsp;
Ultraviolet LLC program. As the name suggests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:16.400
the program aimed to upgrade traditional&nbsp;
photolithography to use extreme ultraviolet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.600
light. ASML did end up figuring out this&nbsp;
technology and customers far preferred it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:26.320
And by the end of the century, ASML grew to&nbsp;
be the 2nd largest semiconductor supplier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:33.840
in the world. Unfortunately though, this was&nbsp;
the highest point they would see for a decade.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:35.760
CLIFF DIVE:
By now, we’ve discussed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:40.480
the dot-com crash so many times on this channel&nbsp;
that I’m sure you guys know what went down. Years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:45.200
of exuberant speculation and hopium finally&nbsp;
came crashing down in the early 2000s which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:50.560
crushed the entire stock market and especially&nbsp;
tech companies, and ASML was by no means immune&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:56.480
to this. ASML was expecting a slowdown as the chip&nbsp;
industry often operates in boom and bust cycles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:01.920
but the bust was far worse than they anticipated.&nbsp;
ASML had told their investors that 2001 sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:08.160
should be roughly the same as 2000, so they were&nbsp;
tempering expectations. But, by March of 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:14.080
it became clear that even matching sales was going&nbsp;
to be impossible. ASML froze hiring and focused on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:18.800
just completing existing contracts, but companies&nbsp;
were pulling out of contracts left and right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:25.120
For example, Intel canceled a $100 million order.&nbsp;
By the end of 2001, net sales had nearly halved in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:33.200
comparison to 2000 from 3.1 billion euros to 1.84&nbsp;
billion euros. ASML also put in a painful $422.4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:39.760
million loss. And as you would guess, the stock&nbsp;
graph didn’t look much better. By October of 2002,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.760 --> 00:07:45.600
ASML stock was down a full 90% from their peak&nbsp;
in 2000. Despite the harsh decline, ASML actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:50.240
faired a lot better than the rest of the industry&nbsp;
given that ASML did eventually recover and put&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:55.600
in new all-time highs. But, back in the early&nbsp;
2000s, things were only getting worse for ASML.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.600 --> 00:08:00.400
At the end of 2001, Nikon filed a patent&nbsp;
infringement lawsuit against ASML. Nikon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:04.880
claimed that ASML had infringed upon 7 of their&nbsp;
patents relating to microlithography equipment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.880 --> 00:08:10.080
A couple of months later in April of 2002, ASML&nbsp;
countersued Nikon suggesting that it was Nikon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.080 --> 00:08:15.120
that infringed upon ASML’s patents. This didn’t&nbsp;
work out too well for ASML though as the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:20.400
eventually came to a settlement where ASML paid&nbsp;
Nikon $87 million. Fortunately though, things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:25.280
finally started to turn around for ASML after&nbsp;
this lawsuit. ASML had started shipping out their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:29.840
TWINSCAN systems which offered a revolutionary&nbsp;
dual-stage technology. Instead of measuring and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:35.200
aligning a wafer and then exposing it, Twinscan&nbsp;
systems could do both tasks simultaneously. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.360
technology allowed customers to produce smaller&nbsp;
chips faster and more efficiently than ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:47.200
before. Also, do you remember the EUV technology&nbsp;
that ASML started working on in the late 1990s?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:52.720
Well, they shipped their first EUV lithography&nbsp;
machine called the NXE 3100 in 2010, and they’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:57.920
been a leader in EUV lithography ever since.&nbsp;
Aside from rolling out new technologies, ASML&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:02.880
was slowly acquiring companies in the background.&nbsp;
For example, in 2001, they acquired the Silicon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.960
Valley Group for $1.6 billion. Similarly, in 2007,&nbsp;
they acquired Brion technologies for $270 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:14.160
And in 2013, they acquired Cymer for&nbsp;
$2.5 billion. All these acquisitions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:21.840
allowed ASML to return to their dot-com peak&nbsp;
by mid-2013, but ASML was just getting started.

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.240
ASML TODAY:
Over the past 5 to 7 years, ASML has been doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:31.360
better than ever before because the semiconductor&nbsp;
industry has been doing than ever before due to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:37.280
three main reasons starting with AMD. In 2017, AMD&nbsp;
introduced their Ryzen line of processors which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.280 --> 00:09:42.560
made gaming computers more affordable and powerful&nbsp;
than ever before. This led to more casual gamers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:47.840
entering the PC master race which substantially&nbsp;
increased demand for CPUs and GPUs. Around the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:53.200
same time, Bitcoin was making its first move&nbsp;
towards to moon rising to $20,000. By this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:58.240
it already didn’t make sense to use GPUs to mine&nbsp;
bitcoin. But, it did make sense to use GPUs to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:02.880
mine ethereum. And given how profitable ethereum&nbsp;
mining was, miners basically bought up the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:08.000
GPU market. Seeing how much demand there was&nbsp;
for GPUs, Nvidia decided to significantly hike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:13.360
prices with the launch of the 2000 series, and&nbsp;
these prices have never gone down. And finally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:17.840
to make things even worse, the pandemic brought&nbsp;
with it the supply shortage which has just made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.840 --> 00:10:21.760
it even easier for suppliers to hike prices&nbsp;
and increase profit margins. Considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:26.320
this semiconductor boom, I don’t think you’d be&nbsp;
too surprised to hear that ASML’s revenues and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:32.240
profits have grown severalfold. In the mid-2010s,&nbsp;
ASML was pulling in $6 to $7 billion in revenue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.240 --> 00:10:38.240
every year. In the most recent 12 month period,&nbsp;
they pulled in over $20 billion. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:43.520
in the mid-2010s, ASML was pulling in about&nbsp;
$1.5 billion in net profit every year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.520 --> 00:10:48.880
In the most recent 12 months period, ASML&nbsp;
pulled in $6.4 billion in net profit. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:54.320
things are going quite well for ASML right now,&nbsp;
but as we saw in 2000, it doesn’t take that long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.320 --> 00:10:59.120
for things to turn around. The supply shortage is&nbsp;
not going to last forever, cryptocurrencies are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.120 --> 00:11:03.920
moving towards proof of stake which eliminates&nbsp;
the need for GPUs and ASICs, and it’s just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:09.120
matter of time until the new influx of gamers due&nbsp;
to Ryzen starts to stagnate. So, the next decline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:15.440
is just a matter of when not if. But, regardless&nbsp;
of what decline comes around, it’s clear that ASML&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.440 --> 00:11:21.120
isn’t going anywhere anytime soon. And for all of&nbsp;
our sakes, that’s probably a good thing. Where you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:27.040
guys familiar with ASML? Comment that down below.&nbsp;
Also, drop a like if you’re thankful for ASML.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.040 --> 00:11:30.240
And of course, consider checking out our&nbsp;
international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.240 --> 00:11:34.080
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
